    12	# Design analysis for the fused variant
    13	# a) Concrete behavior:
    14	#    Original: scattered pieces keep their original body colors, then simply translate
    15	#    onto matching red ports and keep those colors in the output.
    16	#    Fused: the input body colors are no longer arbitrary. They must follow one fixed
    17	#    layer palette in attachment order: blue -> green -> orange -> teal -> gray -> pink.
    18	#    After assembly, the same pieces are recolored with a second ordered palette:
    19	#    maroon -> pink -> gray -> blue -> teal -> green. Example with three pieces:
    20	#    input colors [blue, green, orange] imply "attach first/second/third"; output colors
    21	#    [maroon, pink, gray] confirm that same order after translation.
    22	# b) Complexity calibration:
    23	#    A full tree plus free per-piece colors would hide the new rule inside geometry. To
    24	#    keep the color logic visible in 2-4 examples, the generator builds a bent chain:
    25	#    there is exactly one frontier connector pair at each step, and every sample reuses
    26	#    the same ordered input/output palettes. Geometry still varies, but the color cue is
    27	#    stable and repeated across samples.
    28	# c) New assumptions vs. the original solver:
    29	#    - Non-red overlaps are never legal, even if colors match. The original helper allowed
    30	#      same-color overlap; this fused rule cannot rely on that because colors are semantic.
    31	#    - The non-anchor pieces use the first N colors of INPUT_LAYER_COLORS as a prefix.
    32	#    - During assembly there are exactly two unpaired red cells before each non-final step
    33	#      and zero after the last step. The generator guarantees this by constructing a chain.
    34	# d) Placement legality:
    35	#    Generation remains output-first. Nodes are placed in assembled coordinates first,
    36	#    checked for bbox fit, boundary fit, red-pair counts, and single-component output, then
    37	#    scattered with padding so input pieces do not touch.
    38	# e) Visual observability:
    39	#    Humans can see that input colors are ordered cues, because the same prefix palette is
    40	#    reused across examples and each color appears on a whole sprite rather than tiny marks.
    41	#    The output shows a different ordered palette, so the recoloring is obvious and not
    42	#    confusable with the original task.
    43	# f) Visual cleanliness:
    44	#    Only one layer of semantics is added per stage: input uses one solid body color per
    45	#    piece, output uses one replacement body color per piece, while anchor stays yellow and
    46	#    connectors stay red. No extra overlays are introduced.
    47	# g) Inferability of color changes:
    48	#    The mapping is structured by step index, not by arbitrary sprite identity. Because the
    49	#    chain order is color-coded in the input, the recoloring rule is recoverable from a few
    50	#    examples without hidden numeric conventions.
